1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,915
Here is a limit that looks scary.

2
00:00:02,915 --> 00:00:08,027
As x tends to two, x squared plus
three x, times x minus one, all over

3
00:00:08,027 --> 00:00:09,068
x plus two.

4
00:00:09,468 --> 00:00:13,853
Creeping up on two with a table,
value by value, would take ages.

5
00:00:14,303 --> 00:00:17,724
Aarav thought about this while
cleaning his bicycle chain.

6
00:00:17,724 --> 00:00:20,301
He takes the chain apart, link by link.

7
00:00:20,651 --> 00:00:22,353
Each link is easy to clean.

8
00:00:22,353 --> 00:00:24,273
Then he puts them back together.

9
00:00:24,273 --> 00:00:26,297
Can we do the same with a limit?

10
00:00:26,797 --> 00:00:27,700
Yes, we can.

11
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:30,959
The rules that let us do it are
called the algebra of limits.

12
00:00:31,609 --> 00:00:33,011
First, two tiny limits.

13
00:00:33,011 --> 00:00:34,623
They are the building blocks.

14
00:00:34,973 --> 00:00:38,023
The limit of x, as x
tends to a, is just a.

15
00:00:38,023 --> 00:00:41,625
If x creeps towards two, then
x itself gets close to two.

16
00:00:42,025 --> 00:00:44,791
The limit of a constant is that constant.

17
00:00:44,791 --> 00:00:49,189
If a function always gives five,
it stays five, near every point.

18
00:00:49,839 --> 00:00:51,751
Now watch two pieces at work.

19
00:00:52,101 --> 00:00:55,038
Let f of x be x squared plus three x.

20
00:00:55,038 --> 00:00:57,474
And let g of x be x plus two.

21
00:00:57,874 --> 00:01:01,184
As x creeps towards
two, f closes in on ten.

22
00:01:01,184 --> 00:01:03,087
And g closes in on four.

23
00:01:03,487 --> 00:01:04,387
Add them.

24
00:01:04,341 --> 00:01:06,654
f plus g closes in on fourteen.

25
00:01:06,654 --> 00:01:08,311
Exactly ten plus four.

26
00:01:08,711 --> 00:01:09,881
Multiply them.

27
00:01:09,881 --> 00:01:12,199
f times g closes in on forty.

28
00:01:12,199 --> 00:01:13,992
Exactly ten times four.

29
00:01:14,392 --> 00:01:15,428
Divide them.

30
00:01:15,428 --> 00:01:18,247
f over g closes in on two point five.

31
00:01:18,247 --> 00:01:19,945
Exactly ten over four.

32
00:01:20,595 --> 00:01:21,760
This always works.

33
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:23,520
NCERT gives it as a theorem.

34
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:26,331
Suppose the limits of f
and g at a both exist.

35
00:01:26,731 --> 00:01:27,631
Rule one.

36
00:01:27,322 --> 00:01:29,562
The limit of a sum is
the sum of the limits.

37
00:01:30,312 --> 00:01:31,212
Rule two.

38
00:01:30,929 --> 00:01:33,795
The limit of a difference is
the difference of the limits.

39
00:01:34,545 --> 00:01:35,445
Rule three.

40
00:01:35,243 --> 00:01:37,908
The limit of a product is
the product of the limits.

41
00:01:38,658 --> 00:01:39,558
Rule four.

42
00:01:39,407 --> 00:01:42,513
The limit of a quotient is
the quotient of the limits.

43
00:01:42,513 --> 00:01:45,447
But only when the limit
of the bottom is not zero.

44
00:01:46,197 --> 00:01:48,911
And a number in front just comes along.

45
00:01:48,911 --> 00:01:52,435
The limit of k times f is
k times the limit of f.

46
00:01:53,085 --> 00:01:53,985
Quick one.

47
00:01:53,916 --> 00:01:57,717
What is the limit of five x
squared, as x tends to three?

48
00:01:57,717 --> 00:02:00,564
Forty five, or two
hundred and twenty five?

49
00:02:00,564 --> 00:02:01,739
Pause and think.

50
00:02:04,939 --> 00:02:05,864
Forty five!

51
00:02:05,864 --> 00:02:09,647
x squared tends to nine, and
the five just comes along.

52
00:02:09,647 --> 00:02:11,770
Five times nine is forty five.

53
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:13,499
Side by side.

54
00:02:13,499 --> 00:02:17,257
In our example the bottom,
x plus two, tends to four.

55
00:02:17,257 --> 00:02:20,395
Four is not zero, so
the quotient rule works.

56
00:02:20,795 --> 00:02:25,650
But look at x squared minus four,
over x minus two, as x tends to two.

57
00:02:25,650 --> 00:02:27,430
The bottom tends to zero.

58
00:02:27,430 --> 00:02:30,021
Here the quotient rule is switched off.

59
00:02:30,621 --> 00:02:31,728
Now you try.

60
00:02:31,728 --> 00:02:36,669
For one over x, as x tends to
zero, can we use the quotient rule?

61
00:02:36,669 --> 00:02:38,044
Pause and decide.

62
00:02:41,244 --> 00:02:42,144
No!

63
00:02:41,670 --> 00:02:45,106
The bottom tends to zero,
so the rule is switched off.

64
00:02:45,106 --> 00:02:47,348
We must think about it another way.

65
00:02:47,998 --> 00:02:51,766
Now let us crack the scary
limit, one small step at a time.

66
00:02:52,116 --> 00:02:53,016
Step one.

67
00:02:52,968 --> 00:02:54,797
x squared is x times x.

68
00:02:54,797 --> 00:02:58,069
So it tends to two times
two, which is four.

69
00:02:58,819 --> 00:02:59,719
Step two.

70
00:02:59,657 --> 00:03:04,088
So x squared plus three x tends
to four plus six, which is ten.

71
00:03:04,838 --> 00:03:05,752
Step three.

72
00:03:05,752 --> 00:03:07,599
x minus one tends to one.

73
00:03:07,599 --> 00:03:12,158
By the product rule, the top tends
to ten times one, which is ten.

74
00:03:12,908 --> 00:03:13,808
Step four.

75
00:03:13,775 --> 00:03:16,594
The bottom, x plus two, tends to four.

76
00:03:16,594 --> 00:03:19,165
Four is not zero, so we may divide.

77
00:03:19,915 --> 00:03:20,815
Step five.

78
00:03:20,786 --> 00:03:24,400
The limit is ten over four,
which is two point five.

79
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:25,632
No table needed.

80
00:03:26,282 --> 00:03:27,253
Remember this.

81
00:03:27,253 --> 00:03:31,171
Break it up, find each small
limit, and put them back together.

82
00:03:31,871 --> 00:03:33,561
Now, one worked example.

83
00:03:33,911 --> 00:03:39,147
Find the limit, as x tends to one, of
two x cubed minus three x plus four,

84
00:03:39,147 --> 00:03:41,223
all over x squared plus one.

85
00:03:41,723 --> 00:03:42,623
Step one.

86
00:03:42,609 --> 00:03:47,028
x tends to one, so x cubed and
x squared both tend to one.

87
00:03:47,778 --> 00:03:48,678
Step two.

88
00:03:48,545 --> 00:03:52,304
The top tends to two minus
three plus four, which is three.

89
00:03:53,054 --> 00:03:53,954
Step three.

90
00:03:53,934 --> 00:03:57,091
The bottom tends to one
plus one, which is two.

91
00:03:57,841 --> 00:03:58,741
Step four.

92
00:03:58,686 --> 00:04:01,685
Two is not zero, so the
quotient rule works.

93
00:04:02,435 --> 00:04:03,335
Step five.

94
00:04:03,285 --> 00:04:05,248
The limit is three over two.

95
00:04:05,248 --> 00:04:06,400
One point five.

96
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:08,514
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:08,864 --> 00:04:13,411
Find the limit of x squared minus
nine, over x minus three, as x tends

98
00:04:13,411 --> 00:04:14,311
to three.

99
00:04:14,213 --> 00:04:18,584
Many students split it, get zero
over zero, and write: no limit.

100
00:04:18,984 --> 00:04:22,113
But zero over zero only
switches the rule off.

101
00:04:22,113 --> 00:04:24,098
It does not kill the limit.

102
00:04:24,098 --> 00:04:28,848
Factor the top, cancel x minus
three, and x plus three tends to six.

103
00:04:29,498 --> 00:04:30,895
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:30,895 --> 00:04:35,337
We found no past paper question on
just these rules, so no chip lights.

105
00:04:35,337 --> 00:04:38,327
But they are a step inside
most limit questions.

106
00:04:38,927 --> 00:04:40,079
Exam level now.

107
00:04:40,079 --> 00:04:43,165
Use the limits of f and
g to find this limit.

108
00:04:43,165 --> 00:04:44,367
Pause and solve.

109
00:04:49,767 --> 00:04:51,212
The answer is A.

110
00:04:51,212 --> 00:04:52,814
Seventeen over four.

111
00:04:53,314 --> 00:04:54,214
The top.

112
00:04:54,074 --> 00:04:57,883
Two f squared tends to two
times nine, which is eighteen.

113
00:04:57,883 --> 00:05:00,271
Plus g, which tends to minus one.

114
00:05:00,271 --> 00:05:02,353
So the top tends to seventeen.

115
00:05:02,853 --> 00:05:03,786
The bottom.

116
00:05:03,786 --> 00:05:07,708
f minus g tends to three minus
minus one, which is four.

117
00:05:08,208 --> 00:05:11,295
Four is not zero, so
the quotient rule works.

118
00:05:11,295 --> 00:05:13,566
The answer is seventeen over four.

119
00:05:14,166 --> 00:05:15,706
Here is your recap.

120
00:05:15,706 --> 00:05:17,288
Screenshot this card.

121
00:05:20,488 --> 00:05:22,674
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

122
00:05:23,074 --> 00:05:23,974
One.

123
00:05:23,586 --> 00:05:27,480
What is the limit of x plus
seven, as x tends to three?

124
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:29,280
Two.

125
00:05:28,801 --> 00:05:30,961
When is the quotient rule switched off?

126
00:05:31,861 --> 00:05:32,761
Three.

127
00:05:32,493 --> 00:05:37,687
Why is the limit of four x squared,
as x tends to two, equal to sixteen?

128
00:05:38,787 --> 00:05:40,596
Next time: zero over zero.

129
00:05:40,596 --> 00:05:43,426
Is the limit stuck, or is there a way out?

130
00:05:43,426 --> 00:05:47,044
We find out with limits of
polynomial and rational functions.
